<?php
/**
 * Spartrak Header Search.
 *
 * FIGMA SSOT: desktop node 595:14520, mobile node 724:25849.
 *
 *   bar     62px tall (52 on mobile), bg/field #f3f3f4 (bg/surface-subtle
 *           #f9f9f9 on mobile), 1px border/default, radius 100px, gap 8,
 *           padding-inline 16 on the loupe side / 4 on the button side
 *   loupe   node 693:45436, 20px, stroke bound to action/primary #063196
 *   input   Body/Large — thmanyah sans Regular 400 / 18px / lh 1.5,
 *           placeholder in text/muted, aligned to the inline start
 *   button  node 595:14521 — 97x62, action/primary, radius 100px,
 *           padding-inline 14, [label "بحث" 16px Medium inverse, loupe 20px]
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * WHY THIS EMITS SPARTRAK CLASSES AND NOT CORE'S
 * ===========================================================================
 * QA FIX (2026-08-25). The first version of this override kept core's class
 * names (.block-search / .form.minisearch / .field.search / .actions /
 * .action.search) and styled them. On the live site the desktop search
 * rendered nothing like Figma: no navy submit button, a stray icon-font
 * magnifier, and a ~250px-wide box floated right.
 *
 * ROOT CAUSE — the styles-l trap this theme already documents in
 * Magento_Theme/web/css/source/_extend.less. Magento emits the storefront's
 * CSS as two separately-linked files, and `styles-l.min.css`
 * (media="screen and (min-width:768px)") is linked AFTER the merged bundle
 * that contains spartrak-components.css. theme-frontend-blank's
 * Magento_CatalogSearch/web/css/source/_module.less puts its entire desktop
 * search treatment inside `.media-width('min', @screen__m)`:
 *
 *     .block-search        { float: right; width: 250px; padding-left: 15px; }
 *     .block-search .action.search {
 *         .lib-button-icon(@_icon-font-content: @icon-search,
 *                          @_icon-font-text-hide: true);   // hides the label,
 *                                                          // adds a glyph
 *         .lib-button-reset();                             // kills background
 *         position: absolute; right: 10px; top: 0;
 *     }
 *
 * `.block-search .action.search` is (0,3,0) — the SAME specificity as
 * `.spartrak-header__search .action.search`. Equal specificity is broken by
 * source order, and blank's copy is in the file that loads second, so it won
 * every one of those properties on desktop only. No amount of restating
 * declarations in the component layer fixes that; it needed either a
 * higher-specificity selector (a specificity war, forbidden by CLAUDE.md §9)
 * or the rules relocated into _extend.less.
 *
 * The real fix is to stop competing. Spartrak owns this template, so it owns
 * its class names: with no `.block-search` / `.action.search` in the markup,
 * blank's desktop rules simply do not match, and the component file is the
 * only thing styling this component — no override, no !important, no
 * duplicated declarations, and less CSS overall.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * MAGENTO BEHAVIOUR PRESERVED — every hook quickSearch needs is still here
 * ===========================================================================
 * Verified against vendor/magento/module-search/view/frontend/web/js/
 * form-mini.js, which is what `quickSearch` resolves to:
 *
 *   .spartrak-search__form  options.formSelector — the form it binds submit to
 *                           (a class, not the usual #search_mini_form id; see
 *                           the note on the <form> below for why)
 *   #search               the element the widget is initialised on
 *   #search_autocomplete  options.destinationSelector — where results render
 *   button[type="submit"] options.submitBtn, looked up INSIDE the form; the
 *                         widget enables/disables it as the query changes.
 *                         It matches on the attribute, NOT on a class, which
 *                         is why dropping `.action.search` is safe.
 *
 * The `name`, `maxlength`, `role="combobox"`, `aria-autocomplete`,
 * `autocomplete="off"` and `aria-expanded` attributes are core's own, kept
 * verbatim. $block->getChildHtml() still renders the Advanced Search link
 * slot. Autocomplete, submit and the results route are untouched.
 *
 * The block is a plain Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template (declared in
 * Smartwave_Porto/layout/default.xml), so it has no configProvider /
 * additionalSearchFormData ViewModels — core's own 2.4.8 template would fatal
 * on $configProvider->getSearchHelperData(). The search helper is therefore
 * reached the same way Porto reaches it, which is the arrangement already
 * proven in production here.
 *
 * TWO PLACEHOLDERS: Figma specifies different placeholder copy per breakpoint
 * — the long string at 1440 and the short "ابحث هنا.." at 440. Placeholder
 * text cannot be swapped in CSS, so both are emitted as attributes and
 * spartrakUtilityHeader picks one with matchMedia. They stay real
 * translatable strings (theme i18n/ar_EG.csv) rather than literals in JS.
 *
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 */
$helper = $this->helper('Magento\Search\Helper\Data');

/**
 * SUGGESTIONS ENDPOINT (2026-08-27) — Spartrak_Search, not core's.
 *
 * Figma's suggestions panel (desktop node 864:8879, mobile 647:53929) leads
 * with a result count and a rail of PRODUCT cards. Magento's own
 * `search/ajax/suggest` returns Autocomplete items only — {title,
 * num_results} — with no product behind them, so that endpoint cannot answer
 * this design without the products being invented (CLAUDE.md §5 forbids it).
 *
 * Spartrak_Search runs a real catalogsearch query and returns the count, the
 * products and core's own autocomplete terms together, already rendered.
 * See that module's README for why it returns HTML rather than JSON.
 */
$suggestUrl = $block->getUrl('spartrak_search/ajax/suggest', ['_secure' => $block->getRequest()->isSecure()]);

// The store's own minimum. Passed to the widget so the client stops short of
// a request the endpoint would refuse anyway — the endpoint re-checks it.
$minQueryLength = (int) $helper->getMinQueryLength();
?>
<div class="spartrak-search">
    <?php
    /**
     * NO id="search_mini_form" — deliberately.
     *
     * Mageplaza_Search ships this in its own
     * view/frontend/web/css/source/_module.less:
     *
     *     .media-width('min', @screen__m) {
     *         #search_mini_form { width: 374px; float: right; }
     *     }
     *
     * An ID selector is (1,0,0) and beats ANY class selector no matter how
     * many classes are stacked or which file loads later — so no amount of
     * component CSS could have undone that 374px float, and out-specifying it
     * would have meant an ID or !important of our own. Both are the
     * specificity war CLAUDE.md §9 forbids.
     *
     * Dropping the id removes the match instead. Verified safe: nothing in
     * app/ references #search_mini_form (only Magefan's unrelated
     * #blog_search_mini_form), and core's quickSearch takes the form from
     * `options.formSelector`, which is set to this form's class below.
     *
     * Mageplaza's own frontend search blocks are already removed in this
     * theme's Magento_Theme/layout/default.xml; its stylesheet still compiles
     * into the bundle because the module is enabled, and `_module.less` is
     * collected by @magento_import rather than a <css src>, so it cannot be
     * dropped with <remove>. Not matching it is the clean way out.
     */
    ?>
    <form class="spartrak-search__form" action="<?= $block->escapeUrl($helper->getResultUrl()) ?>" method="get">
        <?php /* Decorative loupe, node 693:45436. Emitted first so RTL places
                 it at the inline start (Figma x=981 of 1017, the right edge)
                 — see header.phtml on child order. Purely ornamental; the
                 input carries the accessible name. */ ?>
        <span class="spartrak-search__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>

        <div class="spartrak-search__field">
            <?php
            /**
             * `spartrakSearchSuggest` REPLACES core's `quickSearch` here.
             *
             * Both render into #search_autocomplete, so running the two
             * together would put competing renderers in one container. The
             * Spartrak widget owns suggestions; SUBMISSION is untouched and
             * stays native — this is a real <form action> with a real submit
             * button, so Enter and the Search button work exactly as before,
             * and still work with JavaScript off, which quickSearch never
             * offered.
             *
             * What quickSearch also did and is deliberately not replaced:
             * enabling/disabling the submit button as the query changes. Figma
             * (node 595:14521) draws the button in one state only, so there is
             * nothing to reproduce.
             */
            ?>
            <input id="search"
                   data-mage-init='{"spartrakSearchSuggest":{
                        "url":"<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($suggestUrl) ?>",
                        "minLength":<?= /* @noEscape */ $minQueryLength ?>,
                        "panelSelector":"#search_autocomplete"}
                   }'
                   type="text"
                   name="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($helper->getQueryParamName()) ?>"
                   value="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($helper->getEscapedQueryText()) ?>"
                   placeholder="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr(__('Search by part number, part name, brand, or model....')) ?>"
                   data-placeholder-compact="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr(__('Search here..')) ?>"
                   aria-label="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr(__('Search')) ?>"
                   class="spartrak-search__input"
                   maxlength="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr($helper->getMaxQueryLength()) ?>"
                   role="combobox"
                   aria-haspopup="false"
                   aria-autocomplete="both"
                   aria-expanded="false"
                   autocomplete="off"/>
            <?php /* Starts `hidden` so an un-hydrated page can never show an
                     empty bordered box; the widget clears the attribute when it
                     has something to put in it. */ ?>
            <div id="search_autocomplete" class="spartrak-search__autocomplete" hidden></div>
            <?php
            /**
             * $block->getChildHtml() is deliberately NOT called here.
             *
             * Core's own form.mini.phtml renders it at this point, which is
             * what emitted the "Advanced Search" link that was leaking out
             * below the search pill on the live header. Figma's search bar
             * (node 595:14520) contains exactly three things — the loupe, the
             * input and the submit button — and no secondary link.
             *
             * Nothing else is lost: this theme's `top.search` block is declared
             * with no children of its own (see Smartwave_Porto/layout/
             * default.xml), so the advanced-search link was the only child, and
             * it remains reachable at its own /catalogsearch/advanced route.
             */
            ?>
        </div>

        <?php /* Node 595:14521. `type="submit"` is what quickSearch finds —
                 no class dependency. The label is visible here (Figma shows
                 "بحث"); core and Porto visually hide it. The button's
                 trailing loupe is a ::after in CSS, because in Figma's LTR
                 order the icon comes first and therefore trails once
                 reversed for RTL. */ ?>
        <button type="submit"
                class="spartrak-search__submit"
                title="<?= $block->escapeHtmlAttr(__('Search')) ?>">
            <span><?= $block->escapeHtml(__('Search')) ?></span>
        </button>
    </form>
</div>
